The Java policy bit mapping agrees with Lean: choose accepts masks 0, 2 and 3, finish only mask 3. The accumulator uses strict comparison, validates count/order/null/negative cost, and latches invalid evaluation. Both metric consumers pass each candidate to this accumulator and only consume its result after their full loop. I source-compiled `BridgePolicies`, `VerifiedPoolSelection`, `LiveFeedback`, `AstFeedback`, and `PoolBridgeSelfTest` into `build/b03-astra-b/classes`, with existing engine and vendor dependencies on the classpath. Execution passed 5,462 exhaustive pools and 52,895 checks. This avoids relying on cached versions of the four reviewed Java implementation classes, while still trusting their dependency classes. The eight focused Python tests also passed, including both policy mutations in both metric paths. Mutation tests provide tested consumption evidence; they do not prove arbitrary Java executions.

The finite verifier emits a theorem for every valuation, including rejected rows; checks the complete expected theorem inventory and empty axiom dependencies; compares generated kernels to the export; and executes the actual kernel code against every expected Boolean output. The saved `build/bridge-first-check/report.json` records 9,224 checked rows, 9,224 JavaScript valuations and eight Java valuations, with malformed inputs and a deliberately false tie row rejected. I inspected that prior report and verifier rather than claiming to have rerun the entire 9,224-row verifier in this review. The targeted tests additionally reject altered Java policy text and malformed table inventories. I found no executable false-green counterexample inside this finite typed Boolean boundary.

Trust remains in the Lean kernel and installed library/imported proof objects, exporter and Python generator/verifier, Boolean encoding and enumeration, JavaScript/JVM/javac execution, dependency classes, and process/filesystem/hash semantics. Empty theorem axiom lists do not remove that computing base. Excluded obligations remain raw input decoding and field extraction, universal host loops and asynchronous bookkeeping, integer cost computation and numeric correspondence, semantic distance and full algorithm correspondence, Alloy parsing/normalization, trace replay and disclosure. The large-Nat probe is a model witness, not a Java arithmetic theorem. B03 does not close the original obligations, and this review does not expand its scope. No frozen input was edited; no network, web, elan, installation or API was used.
